WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily Episode 7. I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.240
Today is June 18th, 2026. Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:15.660
Raptor 3 engine shutdowns during boostback keep Starship reuse timelines uncertain even after

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.920
the redesign. Several Raptor 3 engines stop operating less than 20 seconds into the boostback phase

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.760
on the most recent test flight. The engine iteration was intended to resolve earlier reliability shortfalls

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.360
on Starship vehicles. Early data from the flight shows the shutdowns occurred before the booster

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.940
could complete its return profile. This continues the open question of whether production rate engines

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:46.320
can sustain the repeated high-stress cycles required for rapid reuse. Observers will watch the next

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.360
static-fire campaign for evidence that the issue has been isolated and corrected. While the booster

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:58.280
team works the engine margins, the upper-stage side of the constellation keeps moving forward. SpaceX

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.100
launched three bluebird satellites on a Falcon 9 from a Florida site. The bluebird spacecraft

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:10.660
support expanded direct-to-cell coverage as part of Starlink constellation growth. The launch used

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:16.400
a Falcon 9 booster on a flight that added to the vehicle's reuse count. This flight contributes

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.940
to the tracked question of how quickly the constellation can scale subscriber capacity.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.640
Next data point will be confirmation of initial laser link connections between the new satellites

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.280
and the existing plane. As Starlink hardware multiplies in orbit, SpaceX is quietly studying what

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:40.120
else can ride the same network. SpaceX is assessing whether placing data center hardware in orbit can

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:46.780
deliver lower latency and power costs than ground facilities. Early analysis focuses on radiation

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:53.600
hardening, thermal rejection, and launch mass economics for server racks. The study ties directly

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.380
to Starlink's existing laser-link network for data movement between nodes. Cost for watt delivered

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:05.500
to orbit remains the central unknown. Follow-on work will compare projected power budgets against

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:12.180
terrestrial hyper-scale benchmarks. That long-term infrastructure thinking sits alongside near-term

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.000
competitive developments. Crews have started reconstruction of the new Glenn launch pad damaged

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.260
in the test explosion three weeks ago. Blue origins still targets a return to flight before the end

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:30.840
of 2026. The incident removed a near-term heavy lift option from several manifests while repairs

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.660
proceed. This development affects the competitive landscape for large national security and science

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:42.320
payloads. Watch for the first hot fire test on the rebuilt pad to gauge schedule recovery.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.500
While pad work proceeds elsewhere, a separate on-orbit servicing effort just secured funding.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.340
Quantum Space won a Pentagon contract to develop a fuel transfer spacecraft

00:02:53.340 --> 00:03:00.340
for delivery to the US Space Force by 2028. The vehicle is designed to extend the operational life

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.800
of satellites already in orbit. This contract advances on-orbit servicing capability outside the

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.640
Starship Refueling Architecture. It provides a benchmark for how quickly dedicated tank or hardware

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.800
can reach operational status. The next milestone will be the critical design review for the propellant

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:25.720
transfer system. SPCX has continued to trade lower since the initial surge following the June 12 listing.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.440
The move reflects normal post-IPO price discovery rather than any new operational disclosure.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.420
Volume remains elevated as early shareholders adjust positions. The dual-class structure keeps

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:45.560
voting control with Musk regardless of daily price swings. The next material update will arrive with

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:51.940
the first quarterly filing as a public company. Coverage of the SEX debut emphasizes the company's

00:03:51.940 --> 00:04:02.420
simultaneous work on reusable boosters, satellite constellations, and crew vehicles. The

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:08.200
launch operations remain in investment mode. Analysts note that sustained progress on raptor

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:14.540
production rates will determine whether launch margins improve. The listing itself does not alter day-to-day

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:20.360
hardware development schedules. Watch for the first earnings release to quantify how quickly

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:27.260
Starship flight cadence can scale. Musk acquired additional Tesla shares following a $116 billion

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:33.580
options exercise. The timing has renewed discussion of possible structural links between Tesla and

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:41.240
SpaceX. No formal merger proposal has been filed with regulators. The move occurs while SpaceX operates

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:48.940
as a standalone public company under the SPCX ticker. Investors will monitor any subsequent cross-company

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:56.140
filings for governance details. Observers on X noted that the blue origin pad rebuild timeline still

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:03.100
leaves a gap in heavy lift availability through at least mid-2027. Several accounts highlighted the

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:09.560
quantum space refueling contract as evidence that dedicated tanker spacecraft are advancing on parallel

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:16.240
pass to Starship. Discussion around the raptor 3 test focused on whether the shutdowns were commanded

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:23.900
or uncommanned with no official SpaceX statement yet. Engineers compared the SLC-6 tower removal to

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:30.720
earlier Vandenberg pad modifications that enabled Falcon 9 operations. One thread examined the

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.600
orbital compute study and questioned the radiation shielding mass penalty versus ground-based alternatives.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.840
Worst keeping an eye on is the fact that raptor 3 engines experienced multiple early shutdowns

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.140
during the boost back segment of the latest test. Undercutting the expectation that the redesign

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.700
would deliver the reliability needed for routine booster recovery. The failures occurred well

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.280
before the vehicle reached the condition required for tower catch or rapid turnaround. Resolution

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:05.080
depends on root cause data from the flight recorder and the outcome of the next static fire series.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.080
Until those tests closed the loop, the timeline for full and rapid reuse remains extended.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.760
On the AI front, SpaceX is evaluating the cost effectiveness of placing computing hardware directly

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.680
in orbit, focusing on power, thermal and launch mass trade-offs that would determine whether orbital

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:30.940
data centers can compete with terrestrial facilities. The analysis links to Starlink's laser-link

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:37.260
network for inter-satellite data routing. Separately, the DOJ motion to dismiss the environmental suit

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:44.240
against XAI's Memphis data center cites national security considerations tied to advanced compute

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.720
capacity, though the filing does not reference any SpaceX hardware. From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:56.960
the raptor 3 shutdowns during boost back point to a classic rocket engine reliability problem of

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.740
maintaining stable combustion and turbo pump operation, while the vehicle is still under high dynamic

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:08.900
pressure and changing acceleration. Each engine must deliver consistent chamber pressure and mixture

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:15.560
ratio across a wide throttle range, so any deviation in propellant flow or cooling can trigger an

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:21.440
automatic cutoff to protect the hardware. The redesign aim to protect the margin on these parameters,

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:27.620
yet the early terminations show the achieved margin remains insufficient for the full boost back profile.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:34.060
If the root cause traces to a sensor trip or a real hardware limit, the fix will likely involve

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:40.380
either tighter manufacturing tolerances on the propellant valves or a revised control law that

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:46.420
allows brief excursions without shutdown. Either path increases the idiot index of the engine,

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:52.860
the ratio of finished cost to raw material cost because added sensors, valves, or software

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.460
validation all add expense without changing the underlying metal and propellant.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.440
Closing that gap is essential before the engine can support the hundreds of flights

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:08.800
per booster that Starship economics require. The next concrete step is a dedicated engine test that

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.040
reproduces the exact mixture ratio and pressure condition seen at the moment of cutoff.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:19.620
Data from that test will determine whether the issue is solved at the component level or whether a

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.640
broader architecture change such as a different turbo pump layout is needed. Until that loop is closed,

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:29.560
the open question of rapid reuse cadence stays unresolved.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:38.280
And a quick market note, SPCX is at $191.82, down 1.7%.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.980
Before we go, watch for the next static fire series to show whether the Raptor 3 margins have

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:50.160
improved enough for a full boost back profile. And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:57.120
SPCX is trading at $191.82, down 1.7%.

